1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,150
Every song you’ve ever heard through headphones

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:04,367
came from a tiny disc,

3
00:00:04,117 --> 00:00:06,303
shaking back and forth up to 20,000

4
00:00:06,053 --> 00:00:07,133
times a second.

5
00:00:08,083 --> 00:00:11,133
This is an ordinary pair of over-ear headphones.

6
00:00:10,883 --> 00:00:13,123
Soft cushions, a band, two cups.

7
00:00:12,873 --> 00:00:14,889
But peel back the ear cushion,

8
00:00:14,639 --> 00:00:16,831
and inside each cup sits a small,

9
00:00:16,581 --> 00:00:18,597
round machine called a driver.

10
00:00:21,447 --> 00:00:22,893
Lift its layers apart,

11
00:00:22,643 --> 00:00:24,577
and the driver becomes a stack.

12
00:00:24,327 --> 00:00:25,861
A thin diaphragm on top,

13
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:27,252
the voice coil beneath it,

14
00:00:27,002 --> 00:00:28,108
then the magnet,

15
00:00:27,858 --> 00:00:29,177
all held in a frame.

16
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:30,656
In many over-ear headphones,

17
00:00:30,406 --> 00:00:33,297
the whole thing is about forty millimetres across.

18
00:00:33,047 --> 00:00:34,156
Around its edge,

19
00:00:33,906 --> 00:00:36,516
a soft, flexible surround lets the diaphragm

20
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:39,091
move freely, while keeping it perfectly centred.

21
00:00:40,841 --> 00:00:42,801
Start at the bottom, with the magnet.

22
00:00:42,551 --> 00:00:44,104
It’s often neodymium,

23
00:00:43,854 --> 00:00:47,701
a rare-earth magnet that’s remarkably strong for its size.

24
00:00:47,451 --> 00:00:50,594
Its field is focused into a narrow, ring-shaped gap.

25
00:00:51,644 --> 00:00:53,865
Into that gap drops the voice coil,

26
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:55,723
a tiny spool of fine copper wire,

27
00:00:55,473 --> 00:00:57,524
attached to the diaphragm above.

28
00:00:57,274 --> 00:00:59,521
Your music arrives as a tiny electric

29
00:00:59,271 --> 00:01:02,004
current, changing thousands of times a second.

30
00:01:01,754 --> 00:01:04,112
When that signal flows through the coil,

31
00:01:03,862 --> 00:01:05,587
it becomes an electromagnet.

32
00:01:06,287 --> 00:01:08,073
Now the coil and the magnet push

33
00:01:07,823 --> 00:01:09,417
and pull against each other.

34
00:01:09,167 --> 00:01:10,288
Current one way,

35
00:01:10,038 --> 00:01:11,867
and the coil springs forward.

36
00:01:11,617 --> 00:01:14,037
Reverse the current, and it’s pulled back.

37
00:01:13,787 --> 00:01:15,733
Music is an alternating signal,

38
00:01:15,483 --> 00:01:17,593
so the coil shakes back and forth,

39
00:01:17,343 --> 00:01:19,289
exactly in time with the sound.

40
00:01:20,589 --> 00:01:23,065
And because the coil is fixed to the diaphragm,

41
00:01:22,815 --> 00:01:24,201
the diaphragm moves too,

42
00:01:23,951 --> 00:01:25,669
pushing and pulling on the air.

43
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:28,221
Those tiny pressure waves travel into your ear,

44
00:01:27,971 --> 00:01:29,687
and you hear them as music.

45
00:01:30,387 --> 00:01:32,584
A deep bass note moves it slowly,

46
00:01:32,334 --> 00:01:34,237
as few as 20 times a second.

47
00:01:33,987 --> 00:01:36,208
The highest notes we can hear move

48
00:01:35,958 --> 00:01:37,657
it 20,000 times a second.

49
00:01:37,407 --> 00:01:39,188
So a good diaphragm has to be

50
00:01:38,938 --> 00:01:40,033
light and stiff,

51
00:01:39,783 --> 00:01:42,146
to keep up without bending out of shape.

52
00:01:43,646 --> 00:01:45,196
And there are two drivers,

53
00:01:44,946 --> 00:01:45,996
one in each cup,

54
00:01:45,746 --> 00:01:47,896
each fed its own channel of the music.

55
00:01:47,646 --> 00:01:49,640
That’s what lets a guitar sound like

56
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:50,512
it’s on your left,

57
00:01:50,262 --> 00:01:52,256
and a voice sit right in the middle.

58
00:01:52,956 --> 00:01:54,416
Here’s something lovely.

59
00:01:54,166 --> 00:01:56,004
Run the whole thing backwards,

60
00:01:55,754 --> 00:01:57,486
and it becomes a microphone.

61
00:01:57,236 --> 00:01:58,844
Sound moves the diaphragm,

62
00:01:58,594 --> 00:02:01,037
the coil moves through the magnet’s field,

63
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:02,551
and a tiny current flows out.

64
00:02:03,902 --> 00:02:05,492
Put it all back together.

65
00:02:05,242 --> 00:02:06,395
The signal flows,

66
00:02:06,145 --> 00:02:07,191
the coil pulls,

67
00:02:06,941 --> 00:02:08,307
the diaphragm pushes,

68
00:02:08,057 --> 00:02:10,272
and the air carries the music to you.

69
00:02:10,022 --> 00:02:12,440
All inside something that fits in the palm

70
00:02:12,190 --> 00:02:13,112
of your hand.

71
00:02:12,862 --> 00:02:13,910
The same idea,

72
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,304
scaled up, drives most of the loudspeakers

73
00:02:16,054 --> 00:02:17,045
in the world.

74
00:02:18,095 --> 00:02:20,225
If you enjoyed this, subscribe.

75
00:02:19,975 --> 00:02:23,085
Next time, we’ll open up another everyday machine.

